such as have several words in <a class="xref" href="vol3.html#article-COMMON">common</a>; but which, by the addition of other words, convey an opposite, at least a different meaning. Such are those,

\[
\begin{align*}
&\text{Et <a class="xref" href="vol3.html#article-CANIS">canis</a>} \quad \text{in silva venatur} \\
&\text{Inpsus nutritur} \quad \text{Omnia servat} \\
&\text{<a class="xref" href="vol3.html#article-CONCORDIA">Concordia</a>, a town of <a class="xref" href="vol5.html#article-ITALY">Italy</a>, in the <a class="xref" href="vol4.html#article-DUCHY">duchy</a> of <a class="xref" href="vol7.html#article-MIRANDOLA">Mirandola</a>; seated on the river Sechia, 5 miles west of Mirandola, and 15 miles south-east of <a class="xref" href="vol6.html#article-MANTUA">Mantua</a>; subject to the <a class="xref" href="vol5.html#article-HOUSE">house</a> of <a class="xref" href="vol2.html#article-AUSTRIA">Austria</a>. E. <a class="xref" href="vol0.html#article-LONG">Long</a>. 11. 22. N. Lat. 44. 52.}
\end{align*}
